## Rule 4: Microservices Cautions ⚠️

### Service Boundaries
```
⚠️ NEVER directly access another service's database
⚠️ NEVER share entity classes between services
✅ Use WebClient/REST calls for inter-service communication
✅ Each service owns its data exclusively
```

### Configuration Management
```
⚠️ NEVER hardcode service URLs (use discovery-service)
⚠️ NEVER commit secrets to config-repo
✅ Use Config Server for externalized config
✅ Use bootstrap.properties for config-server connection
```

Example:
```java
// ❌ WRONG - Hardcoded URL
private String productServiceUrl = "http://localhost:8082";

// ✅ CORRECT - Use service discovery
@LoadBalanced
private WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder;
// Call via service name: "http://product-service/api/products"
```

### Asynchronous Messaging (RabbitMQ)
```
⚠️ NEVER assume message delivery is instant
⚠️ NEVER put critical sync logic in message handlers
✅ Design for eventual consistency
✅ Implement idempotent message handlers
✅ Add dead-letter queues for failed messages
```

### Distributed Transactions
```
⚠️ NEVER use @Transactional across multiple services
⚠️ NEVER expect ACID guarantees across services
✅ Use Saga pattern for multi-service operations
✅ Implement compensation logic for rollbacks
```

### Circuit Breaker & Resilience
```java
@CircuitBreaker(name = "product-service", fallbackMethod = "getProductFallback")
@Retry(name = "product-service")
public Product getProduct(Long id) {
    return webClient.get()
        .uri("/api/products/{id}", id)
        .retrieve()
        .bodyToMono(Product.class)
        .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))  // Always set timeout!
        .block();
}
```

